\description{
Computes connectedness between management units (MUs) under the contrast
approach via Mixed Model Equations (MME). The function returns two
complementary metrics for all MU pairs: the Coefficient of Determination
(\code{CD}) and the Prediction Error Variance of Differences (\code{PEVD}).
}
\details{
The connectedness analysis can be based on a pedigree-derived inverse
relationship matrix (\code{Ainv}), a genomic inverse relationship matrix (\code{Ginv}),
a combined pedigree-genomic inverse relationship matrix (\code{Hinv}), or a user-supplied
inverse kernel.

For each pair of management units \eqn{(i, j)}, the contrast assigns weight
\eqn{1/n_i} to animals in MU \eqn{i} and \eqn{-1/n_j} to animals in MU
\eqn{j}, where \eqn{n_i} and \eqn{n_j} are the numbers of target animals in
each unit.

\code{PEVD} measures the prediction error variance of these contrasts, whereas
\code{CD} rescales the contrast information relative to the denominator implied by
the kernel actually used in the analysis. The object component \code{qK} therefore
refers generically to the denominator under \code{A}, \code{G}, \code{H}, or a custom kernel.

If \code{year_window} is supplied, connectedness is computed in two steps. First,
the function identifies MUs of interest using the temporal activity criterion.
Second, it computes CD and PEVD for contrasts among target animals from those
MUs using the full set of records in \code{data}.

With \code{target_scope = "window"} (default), the function answers: among
animals recorded in the specified time window from active MUs, how connected
are those MUs when evaluated using all information available in the system?
With \code{target_scope = "selected_mus"}, the function answers: among MUs
selected as active in the specified time window, how connected are those MUs
when all animals from those MUs are used as targets and all information
available in the system is used?

The MME is always built from all records supplied in \code{data}. The temporal
arguments determine the set of reported MUs and the set of animals receiving
non-zero weights in the contrast vector. Under \code{target_scope = "window"},
animals from a pair of MUs inside the window receive weights \code{1 / n_i} and
\code{-1 / n_j}, respectively, whereas all other animals receive weight zero in
the contrast but may still contribute information through the MME.

The \code{"full_mme"} backend directly factorizes the full MME matrix
\code{[X'X X'Z; Z'X Z'Z + lambda Kinv]}. The \code{"schur"} backend avoids this full
factorization by factorizing \code{Cuu = Z'Z + lambda Kinv} and using the fixed-
effect Schur complement \code{S = X'X - X'Z Cuu^{-1} Z'X}.

The Schur formulation is algebraically independent of the relationship matrix
type. With \code{schur_solver = "auto"}, sparse inverse kernels such as \code{Ainv}
are solved through CHOLMOD via the Matrix package, while dense kernels such as
\code{Ginv} are solved through a dense compiled backend. If the dense Schur
working matrix \code{Cuu^{-1} Z'X} would be very large, \code{"auto"} selects
\code{"cholmod_lowmem"}, which computes the same Schur complement in blocks.
The \code{"eigen_sparse"} solver is retained mainly for diagnostics and
small-scale comparisons.
}
